<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/apartments.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/apartments-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/apartments.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Commercial and HOA Insurance Appraisal in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Larger buildings mean larger estimates, more trades and more room for two reasonable professionals to land far apart. We bring a disciplined, documented valuation to the appraisal panel.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Property types</small><b>Commercial, multifamily, HOA, churches</b></div><div><small>Common perils</small><b>Hail, wind, water, fire</b></div><div><small>Key issue</small><b>Scope across many units</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Commercial and HOA insurance appraisal in Wisconsin covers the claims where a single estimate can run hundreds of pages: a strip center hit by hail, a forty-unit condo association with wind damage across six buildings, an apartment complex with a burst riser, or a church with a slate roof and stained glass. We act as the appraiser for the property owner or association once appraisal is demanded.</p>
<h2>Who we work with</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Condominium associations and HOAs.</strong> Boards responsible for roofs, siding, common areas and building systems under a master policy.</li>
<li><strong>Multifamily owners.</strong> Apartment buildings, from older brick walk-ups to newer garden-style complexes.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial owners.</strong> Offices, retail, light industrial, restaurants and mixed-use buildings.</li>
<li><strong>Houses of worship.</strong> Churches with tall steep roofs, specialty materials and fellowship halls that see heavy use.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why big claims drift apart</h2>
<p>On a single house, two estimators might disagree on a few dozen line items. On a commercial or association loss, the disagreements multiply. A carrier may sample a few roofs and extrapolate. Commercial roofing systems such as EPDM, TPO or modified bitumen may be treated as repairable when the membrane is compromised across the field. Business interruption, extra expense and code requirements for occupied buildings add layers that residential templates never touch.</p>
<h3>Questions boards and owners should ask early</h3>
<ul>
<li>Does the policy have a blanket limit or building-by-building limits?</li>
<li>Is there a separate wind or hail deductible, and how is it calculated?</li>
<li>Does the policy pay replacement cost only after repairs are completed?</li>
<li>Is there an ordinance or law endorsement, and what are its limits?</li>
</ul>
<h2>How we handle a large-loss appraisal</h2>
<p>We inspect every affected building, not a sample, unless both appraisers agree in writing that sampling is reasonable. We organize the estimate by building and system so the other appraiser and any umpire can follow it. Where the dispute is technical, such as whether a membrane can be repaired, we document test cuts, moisture readings and manufacturer guidance.</p>
<p>For associations, we also keep the board informed in language it can share with owners. Boards often have to explain to residents why a claim is taking time, and a clear summary of where the panel stands helps.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Where appraisal stops</b>Appraisal decides the amount of loss. Coverage disputes, questions about how claims were handled and any bad-faith concerns are legal matters for a Wisconsin attorney.</div>
<h2>Working across the state</h2>
<p>We take commercial and association appraisals statewide, from the Fox Valley to the Chippewa Valley. See our <a href="/local-appraisers/green-bay/">Green Bay</a>, <a href="/local-appraisers/appleton/">Appleton</a> and <a href="/local-appraisers/eau-claire/">Eau Claire</a> pages for local context. If hail is the main peril, our <a href="/appraisal-solutions/hail-roof-damage-appraisal/">hail and roof appraisal</a> page goes deeper on roofing disputes.</p>
<p>If your board or ownership group is weighing appraisal, <a href="/contact-us/">reach out</a> with the policy, the carrier’s estimate and any contractor bids. We’ll tell you plainly whether we think appraisal fits, and if a narrower step such as an <a href="/appraisal-solutions/carrier-estimate-review/">estimate review</a> would serve the association better first, we’ll say that too.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can a condo association invoke appraisal without a vote of all owners?</summary><div class="a"><p>That depends on the association’s governing documents. Typically the board acts on behalf of the association on master-policy claims, but check your bylaws.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you handle business interruption in appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Some policies allow business income losses to be appraised and some do not. We review the policy language first and, where appropriate, coordinate with an accountant on the income side.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long does a commercial appraisal take?</summary><div class="a"><p>Larger losses usually take longer than residential claims because of inspection time and estimate size. A multi-building claim can take several months from demand to award.</p></div></details></div>
